00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:05.470 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  hi I'm Nick Gillespie for recent TV  today we're talking with Judge Andrew  Napolitano author of the new book lies  the government told you miss power and  deception in American history judge  thanks for talking pleasure to be with  you Nick whether we're talking about my  book or anything why did you write this  book because the thing that impressed me  the most when I was on the bench as a  judge hearing arguments between the  government and a defendant in a criminal  case or the government and someone who  had been injured by the government in a  civil case was the extent to which the  government would lie and people in the  courtroom members of the jury lawyers  involved in the case would expect the  government to lie you know there are at  least a half dozen Supreme Court  opinions that say the government can lie  tell us what what's one of them well the  context in which the government may lie  is when it's interrogating a potential  defendant the classic modern-day example  is Martha Stewart she's with an FBI  agent there in her lawyer's office the  lawyer says to the FBI agent is my  client a target the FBI agent says no  the FBI agent then says to Martha  Stewart did you buy such and such a  stock on such-and-such a day she says no  they each lied she went to jail for five  months and he got a promotion through  well what is it about the government are  they morally superior to us that they  can lie with impunity  lie with the law behind them and if one  of us would presume to do it to them we  could end up in jail the the government  has institutionalized lying from all men  are created equal  hard for me to to attack that because it  was written by my hero and favorite  president Thomas Jefferson too we don't  torture and about fifteen or sixteen  significant historical lies in between  when I kiddingly told friends I was  writing a book about the government  lying and it was four thousand pages  long many of them said you've got all  the lies in 4,000 pages obviously it's  not four thousand pages it's three  hundred but it shows you how  acculturated we are to the concept of  government laws  let's talk about some of the specific  laws that you run through you say one of  the lies is we don't torture how is that  a lie and what what is destructive not  just about the torture perhaps but the  lie almost all government lying is  destructive because it induces people to  to view the government in a false  environment some lies like LBJ in 1964  I'm the peace candidate or FDR in 1940  your boys will never fight a war on  foreign soil or George Bush in 2004 we  don't torture I'm not spying and they  have weapons of mass destruction those  are the category of lies that induce us  to give power to the government based on  those lies question would we have given  that power to the government had we  known the truth the we don't torture is  particularly offensive a it's it's  incorrect it's a lie and the person who  said it  President Bush must have known that it  was a lie because we now have documents  that we know he saw in which he  authorized torture be it's particularly  horrific because it rejects the notion  that all men are endowed by their  creator with certain inalienable rights  if they cover all those - in torture to  torture someone they reject the notion  of an alien some people would say that  well you know we don't torture American  citizens why from your point of view is  does it not matter if we're torturing  non-citizens or this morning the  Washington Post reported that the CIA  announced that it will target Americans  when they're overseas for assassination  so it's obvious that being an American  is no additional protection but as a  matter of constitutional law the  Constitution protects persons it's not  limited to Americans and persons is not  even limited to good persons it protects  Americans it protects aliens it protects  those legally here protects those  illegally here protects those who wish  us well and those who have caused us  harm it makes no distinction whatsoever  this is absolutely consistent the  constitutional protection of persons  with the Lockean and Jeffersonian and  Augustinian view  and to mystic view that our rights come  from God and our gifts into our humanity  and are as much a part of us as the  fingers on the ends of our hand that  would apply to me to you to George W  Bush to Barack Obama to Khalid Sheikh  Mohammed to Richard Speck to Al Capone  to anybody that the government wants to  restrain for any reason do you think  that natural law is gaining ground or is  receding amongst people who are  concerned with the government amongst  people who have come to realize that we  have rights that are integral to our  humanity it's gaining ground amongst  young people it's gaining ground amongst  the Liberty movement it's gaining ground  amongst reasons viewers listeners and  and readers it is gaining ground in the  government it is not the other day at  Fox I asked Congressman Jim Clyburn the  number three ranking Democrat in the  house congressman Clyburn where in the  Constitution as the government  authorized to regulate healthcare and he  said to me judge most of what we do is  not authorized by the Constitution where  is it prohibited to the government to  regulate healthcare he has turned the  night in his own mind turned the notion  of limited government on his head on its  head and believes that the Congress can  do anything it wants unless it's  specifically prohibited in the  Constitution but the concept of natural  law articulated in the Declaration of  Independence codified in the Bill of  Rights is the opposite of that and that  is Congress may only do what the  Constitution authorizes it to do and  there are areas of human behavior where  Congress may not regulate no matter what  the good there may be that could come  from its proposed regulation let's talk  about a place another lie that speaks to  this Congress shall make no law  abridging free speech this is arguably  the most important right it is and what  are the ways in which you know when you  look around the world today or look  around America today we have much more  access to many more materials is  censorship is abridgement of free speech  and issue and in what way look free  speech was just saved by three black  robe judges in this  city earlier this week when the United  States Court of Appeals for the District  of Columbia Circuit ruled that the  Federal Communications Commission cannot  regulate the Internet they really wanted  to make sure that there was the Fairness  Doctrine on the Internet  I mean if anything is the free  marketplace of ideas today it is the  Internet but this goes way back Nick I  mean think about it the same generation  in some instances the same human beings  who wrote Congress shall make no law  abridging the freedom of speech also  wrote the Alien and Sedition Acts which  made it a crime to criticize everybody  in the government except the  vice-president because it happened to be  Jefferson at the time congressman  Matthew Lyon of Vermont if anybody is  from New Orleans Chicago or New Jersey  they'll appreciate this story  congressman Lyon from Vermont was  prosecuted for criticizing the president  John Adams for being too involved in the  pomp and circumstance of the office but  the most offending thing he said was and  John Adams had a little problem with his  waistline he referred to the President  as his rotundity here's the New Orleans  Chicago New Jersey angle Lyons was  re-elected to Congress from his jail  cell because people were so outraged at  this prosecution but think of it we  fought a war against a king and a  parliament 3,000 miles away because they  they assaulted speech we became a  country and we began to assault speech  Lincoln locked people up for what they  said Woodrow Wilson locked people up for  what they said FDR locked people up for  what they said George W Bush locked  people up for what they said so what  part of shall make no law does the  Congress not understand who did George  Bush lock up for what he said to  librarians in Bridgeport Connecticut  were served with a self written search  warrant authorized by the Patriot Act  that's where an FBI agent and his or her  boss together write a search warrant  without going to a judge as the Fourth  Amendment requires the FBI agent shows  up at this library the librarian is 86  years old he hands her the search  warrant says don't tell anybody about it  she looks at him and says who the hell  are you  she was he was young enough to be her  grandson she hands the search warrant  to her assistant that was considered  speech telling somebody else that she  had received a self written search  warrant the Patriot Act makes it a crime  to tell anyone that you have received a  self written search warrant you can't  tell your spouse in the bedroom  your lawyer in her office your priest in  the confessional you can't even tell a  judge in a public courtroom under oath  that you received it this 86 year old  librarian told her 75 year old assistant  and the government went after them  [Music]

